      <p><b>What it is:</b> <i>Rhodococcus equi</i> (formerly <i>Corynebacterium equi</i>; Greek: <i>rhodon</i> [rose], <i>kokkos</i> [berry], <i>equus</i> [horse]) is an aerobic, non-motile, non-spore-forming, catalase-positive Gram-positive bacterium. It exhibits a striking, time-dependent <b>rod-to-coccus morphogenesis</b>: when grown in liquid culture, it initially appears as slender, pleomorphic, coryneform-like rods with rudimentary branching (4 hours), and subsequently fragments into uniform, spherical coccoid forms (18 to 24 hours).</p>
